WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
Everyone gets the same amount [music] of

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:07.360
time every single day. 86,400 seconds.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:07.860
No more for Elon, no less for you. Same

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:07.960
hours,

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:10.320
>> [music]

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:12.200
>> wildly different results. You've heard

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.800
the productivity advice. Wake up at 5:00

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:16.120
a.m. Cold plunge. Eat your frog. The

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.240
truth is most of that advice, [music]

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:19.880
it's It's performing for the

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:22.080
sake of discipline. I run a large

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:23.600
company. I write. I travel, and I still

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.960
have a life. And one of the questions I

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:27.440
get more than [music] almost anything

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.200
else is the age-old how do you do it

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.785
all? And the truth of the matter is, in

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:32.759
this video, I'm going to give you

00:00:30.785 --> 00:00:34.520
[music] exactly what I do, how I stole

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:36.760
it from a bunch of the smartest CEOs in

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:39.400
the world, and if you take one thing

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:41.040
away from this system today, well, I can

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:43.480
tell you, it'll probably change your

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.480
life.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.840
Get this. I believe your day actually

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:48.680
starts before you even wake in the

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:50.480
morning. I mean, if you wake up and plan

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:52.160
the day that morning, you've actually

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:53.680
already lost. Someone will send you

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:55.440
something urgent, and before you know

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:57.160
it, you're making someone else's needs a

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.800
priority over your own. And things that

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:01.240
could have been brought TO MY ATTENTION

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:03.320
YESTERDAY. YOUR DAY BECOMES reactive

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:04.879
instead of intentional. So, I plan every

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:07.240
day the night before, sometimes even

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:09.440
months before. Non-negotiable. 15

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:10.920
minutes. Sit down. Ask yourself, what is

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:12.720
the most important thing you can do

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:14.280
tomorrow that moves the needle? I have a

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.680
very particular way of deciding the big

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:18.920
objectives and staying on track. Then I

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:21.320
make a list. Not a to-do list, a

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:23.480
priority list. A to-do list is a wish. A

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:25.000
priority list is a plan. A priority list

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:26.680
forces you to decide what matters most

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:30.040
when you can't do everything, and trust

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:30.040
me, you never can.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:33.880
Section two, sleep. I spent years

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:35.280
treating sleep like it was stealing from

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:37.320
me. Turns out I was stealing from

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:38.600
myself. Your prefrontal cortex, that

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:40.640
part of your brain responsible for

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:42.560
judgment, decision-making, impulse

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:44.000
control, well, it takes a hit when

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:46.920
you're sleep deprived. You think you're

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.680
sharp, but your brain is drunk driving.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:49.560
You have decisions on 4 hours of sleep

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:52.040
that you can't even tell what's

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.040
happening.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:56.960
The evening wind down, critical. Little

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.800
under eye patch, skin care, ways to tell

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:00.760
my nervous system the day is over. It's

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.280
time to chill. The transition matters.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:04.440
You can't go from 90 miles an hour to

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:05.720
deep sleep without a runway. The single

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:07.120
highest leverage thing you can do right

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:10.240
now if you want tomorrow to be your best

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:12.560
day, go to sleep.

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:14.920
Section three, morning ritual.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:17.080
I get up early, [music] 5:30, 6:00. And

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:19.800
it's a priority right away to get some

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:21.560
light on my face if it's out. This is

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:23.446
like a free reset for your circadian

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:23.680
clock and it supercharges your brain.

00:02:23.446 --> 00:02:24.920
>> [music]

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:26.840
>> People think making your bed is a sign

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:28.680
of a clean and organized person. Really?

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:30.760
It's giving yourself an easy win. And

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:31.959
wins stack. Every one of those pieces

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:34.360
keeps carrying you forward throughout

00:02:31.959 --> 00:02:36.760
your day. If it's not the sun out, then

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:39.000
it could be a sauna or a walk. I also

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:41.680
like to journal. Not about feelings, but

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:42.720
clarity. No email or Slack. The second

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:44.240
you open email, you're working on

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.240
somebody else's priorities. You just

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:48.120
handed them your sharpest hours.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:49.680
So just get clear on what you want your

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:50.720
day to look like. Your brain's sharper

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:53.000
in those first few hours,

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:55.000
decision-making cleaner, creativity

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:56.480
higher. The first window of your day,

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:57.640
that's not free time. That's the most

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:59.640
expensive real estate in [music] your

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:02.040
schedule. Protect it like it's money

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:03.840
because it is.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:06.200
Section four, deep work.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:08.480
Most important part in the day, the deep

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:10.160
work block. Decision fatigue is real. By

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:12.360
3:00 p.m. your brain has made thousands

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:14.000
of tiny decisions and it's worse at

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:15.920
making the good ones. So I do the work

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.400
that matters when my brain is sharpest.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:19.440
The filter I use for what gets done

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:22.760
during this time is to ask, "Is this

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:24.280
leverage work, support work, or noise?"

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:27.120
Deep work is leverage work and that's

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:31.440
what gets this precious time. It's

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:33.600
strategy, content, sales, writing, big

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:35.200
deal thinking. Support work is when you

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:36.720
feel busy. You're checking boxes, you're

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.720
responding to things, but you're not

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.760
building anything. You're just

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:41.880
maintaining. Give this time to work that

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:43.760
can compound.

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:46.000
Section five, the gym.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:47.840
I do some form of exercise every day. No

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.320
exceptions. Forget nootropics and the

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:51.120
supplement stack. The best single

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:53.182
performance drug I've ever found is

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:53.200
picking up something heavy early.

00:03:53.182 --> 00:03:54.520
>> [music]

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:56.760
>> Almost everything in your professional

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:59.200
life is optimized for typing and

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:00.520
staring. All brain, no body. Almost

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:02.080
nothing requires you to feel your own

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:03.760
strength. [music] When you lift heavy

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:05.440
weights, your problems stop feeling that

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:07.560
big. You're telling your nervous system,

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:09.760
"Hey, we're capable. We can carry heavy

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:11.040
loads." That signal transfers into every

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:12.360
other part of your day. The discipline

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.840
of showing up even when you don't feel

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:15.200
like it, even when you're traveling,

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:16.959
builds a muscle that transfers to

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:18.760
everything else. You prove to yourself

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:20.480
daily that you do what you say you're

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:22.080
going to do. The gym isn't on my

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:23.520
calendar because I have time. It's on my

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:25.240
calendar because I can't afford to skip

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:27.680
it.

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:28.800
Section six, work in person. From here,

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:30.760
I'm handing [music] the filming off to

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:32.400
my team so I can focus on my day. This

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:34.360
is me practicing what I preach. I can't

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:36.680
do it all, so I don't. Outsource to the

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:38.378
pros. Our office gets rolling early. I

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:39.520
strongly believe in in-person work.

00:04:38.378 --> 00:04:41.760
[music] Researchers found that

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:43.840
face-to-face requests are 34 times more

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:44.960
likely to get a yes than email. A bunch

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:46.238
of other studies found that colleagues

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:46.280
who shared physical space

00:04:46.238 --> 00:04:48.160
>> [music]

00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:50.360
>> reference each other's works three to

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:51.480
four times more than those who don't. I

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:53.280
found you just can't get the same

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:54.960
momentum when you're remote. So, our

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:56.800
whole team comes in every day, and we're

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:58.680
growing fast. Second office we've

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:00.640
outgrown in 9 months. This was the size

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:03.320
of my team just 9 months ago. Our first

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:04.919
office was a 3,800 square foot house.

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:06.560
This is a 10,000 square foot office

00:05:04.919 --> 00:05:07.640
building. By the time you're watching

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:10.360
this, we'll already have moved into a

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:12.320
bigger space that's 14,000 square feet

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:13.480
and thousands of feet in the studio.

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:16.000
That's what happens when you keep

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:18.120
building together.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.640
Section seven, appearance. Most days,

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:20.760
filming or hosting event is part of my

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:22.040
schedule, [music] so I want to be camera

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:23.480
ready. Look, I don't actually love

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:25.200
wearing makeup, but that's mostly

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:26.640
because I don't want it to impact what I

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:28.040
can get done in the day. So, when I'm

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:29.480
doing it on my own, I keep it simple.

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:31.440
Otherwise, hair and makeup gets done

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:33.320
during meetings, between decisions, or

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:34.640
alongside reviews. My lesson here is

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:35.720
that efficiency isn't just about what

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:37.919
you're doing, it's about how you're

00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:39.040
doing it. Where can you combine tasks?

00:05:37.919 --> 00:05:40.160
Where can you think completely

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:42.560
differently, even if it's a little

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:44.280
psychotic like me doing this? And nobody

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:45.360
wants to hear this, but how you show up

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:47.480
physically is a decision you're

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:49.880
broadcasting to everyone. Your team

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:51.083
reads it. Other things I stand behind,

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:52.000
the shoes.

00:05:51.083 --> 00:05:53.520
>> [clears throat]

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:55.040
>> Always comfortable. I have long days and

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:57.160
if your feet hurt, your brain is solving

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:58.480
the wrong problem all day. I eat chia

00:05:57.160 --> 00:06:00.560
pudding with fresh fruit. It's one of

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:02.040
the best plant sources of omega-3s. It's

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:03.280
great for your brain function, so bring

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:04.720
it on. I think you should always try to

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:06.680
feed your brain before you ask it to do

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:08.800
tricks. You wouldn't run a Ferrari on a

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:10.360
gas station coffee. And every meeting on

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:13.120
my calendar has a defined objective and

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:15.040
defined end time.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:16.360
Section eight, work that comes to you.

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:18.520
We have a full studio in our office.

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:19.840
Guests come here. We record here. I used

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:21.869
to go to the studio, go to the coffee

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:21.960
shop, drive across town for every shoot.

00:06:21.869 --> 00:06:23.640
>> [music]

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:24.919
>> Now, all in one place. If you have

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:26.760
something you need to do consistently,

00:06:24.919 --> 00:06:30.440
make it come to you. Every commute in

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:32.200
your workflow is a leak. Plug it.

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:34.280
Section nine, scheduling days at the

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:35.919
office. I try to block at least one hour

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:37.040
on my calendar midday to sit at my desk

00:06:35.919 --> 00:06:38.520
and do a full check-in on what's

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:40.919
happening around the company. Make

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:43.520
approvals, complete quick tasks or asks,

00:06:40.919 --> 00:06:44.880
but this, big no-no. I really try to

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:47.080
avoid people streaming in and out of the

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:49.320
office. Got a minute? It's the most

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:51.600
expensive question in in any office. It

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:52.720
never costs a minute, always costs 20.

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:54.360
So, train your team that you're not

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:56.200
immediately available. You're not being

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:58.120
rude, you're focusing.

00:06:56.200 --> 00:07:00.160
So, first, I admit this is not

00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:01.880
foolproof. When someone tries to grab an

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:04.000
unscheduled meeting, my team knows the

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:06.040
answer. Hey, just give me a brief loom

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:07.800
or a Slack message. If we still need to

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:08.960
sync after that, ask my assistant, we'll

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:10.919
help you find 15 minutes on the

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:11.649
calendar. Most of the time, it's not

00:07:10.919 --> 00:07:11.800
necessary.

00:07:11.649 --> 00:07:12.880
>> [music]

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:14.400
>> That's not avoiding people, that's

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:16.560
respecting everyone's time including

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:18.400
yours.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:19.720
Section 10, [music] walking meetings. If

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:21.120
it does require a meeting, here's one of

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:23.200
my favorite ways to do it, a walking

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:25.360
meeting. Get outside. It changes the

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:27.640
conversation. It's loose. People are

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:29.280
honest. Steve Jobs held almost all of

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:31.400
his meetings on foot. There's a reason

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:34.240
for that. Stanford found walking boosts

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:35.680
creative output by over 80%. Even

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:37.320
meetings that need to be virtual, I try

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:38.600
to take on my office treadmill. I walk

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:40.840
as much as I can during the day.

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:42.520
Otherwise, I can't get my steps.

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:43.920
Another meeting rule, keep it small.

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:45.560
Every person you add to a meeting isn't

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:47.280
just another chair, it's another salary

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:48.880
sitting in the room. Look around and

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:50.960
think about the actual dollar cost

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:53.040
adding up. You'll agree. Invite people

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:55.600
whose input is required. Everyone else

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:57.880
gets the TLDR report.

00:07:55.600 --> 00:07:59.160
Section 11, reducing stress. You might

00:07:57.880 --> 00:08:00.800
have found [music] this guy before,

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:02.920
Babar. He's my dog. He comes to the

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:04.600
office. He's a Cavapoo I've had for 6

00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:06.600
years. I swear he can feel my mood. So,

00:08:04.600 --> 00:08:07.960
when I'm stressed, he finds me. When I'm

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:09.440
in the zone, he leaves me alone. He's

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:11.080
always by my side. [music] I stand

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:12.720
behind the research on this. Having an

00:08:11.080 --> 00:08:14.040
animal nearby measurably reduces

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:16.000
cortisol. Babar doesn't know he's a

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:17.040
stress reduction tool, but he is. Do

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:19.000
whatever you have to do to break the

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:19.919
tension. Because stress doesn't just

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:21.760
make [music] you feel bad, it makes you

00:08:19.919 --> 00:08:23.640
stupid. It literally shrinks the part of

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:26.240
your brain that makes good decisions. I

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:28.440
say, walk more, get an animal.

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:30.280
Section 12, offsite meetings. Here's a

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:32.200
line I'll stand behind. Carving out time

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:34.520
to build relationships something you

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:35.680
have to do. I [music] set aside an hour

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:37.880
or so three times a week for an

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:40.039
in-person coffee. This is how I find my

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:41.919
who's. It will help you figure out the

00:08:40.039 --> 00:08:44.039
critical shift from how do I figure this

00:08:41.919 --> 00:08:46.120
out to who already has figured this out

00:08:44.039 --> 00:08:47.480
to who can figure this out for me. This

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:49.040
can change the speed of everything you

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:51.000
do. Catch yourself next time you're

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:52.680
Googling something, instead find the

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:54.920
person who already knows. Relationship

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:56.520
building is a skill. It requires reps.

